# V. Risk factors

# 5.1 Client concentration risk
Companies rely on a relatively small number of clients of large financial institutions, and the loss of or financial difficulties for any major customer may have a significant impact on revenue.

#5.2 Risk of technological subversion
Financial science and technology innovation may change the way investors communicate and backstage processing. Block chain technology, if applied on a large scale in the area of securities settlement and voting, may bypass traditional intermediaries.

#5.3 Regulatory change risks
Companies rely heavily on regulatory agencies such as SEC for proxy voting and investor communications.

#5.4 Risk of declining activity in securities markets
The number of positions held and market participation directly affect the amount of proxy material that needs to be sent. The long-term market downturn may result in fewer disposals.

#5.5 Cybersecurity risk
As an infrastructure provider for processing sensitive financial data, any major security incident could result in loss of trust and regulatory sanctions for clients.

#5.6 Lever and M & As integration risk
The high corporate liability/equity ratio (approximately 1:1) and the continued acquisition (e.g., the completion of the Acolin acquisition in early 2026) may result in a failure to integrate that could delay performance.

